data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR/Cas9 comes from a bacterial immune system that cuts up invading viral DNA. Scientists have adapted it to change chosen letters in an organism's DNA. [1]\n- Guide RNA locates the target DNA, and the Cas9 protein cuts it at that spot. [2]\n- Scientists often use CRISPR to knock out a gene, and increasingly to replace disease-causing mutations using a repair template. [3]\n- Research is often done in mouse embryos or cells grown in dishes, and some teams modify stem cells. [1]\n- Reported applications include eliminating HIV in cell culture [4] and an FDA review of a CRISPR treatment for sickle cell [5]."}

